Sure! Here's a simple JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  const words = sentence.split(" ");
  const adjectives = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    if (words[i].match(/^[a-zA-Z]+$/)) {
      adjectives.push(words[i]);
    }
  }

  const reversedAdjectives = adjectives.reverse();
  const newSentence = reversedAdjectives.join(" ") + " " + words.filter(word => !word.match(/^[a-zA-Z]+$/)).join(" ");

  return newSentence;
}

const inputSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const outputSentence = switchAdjectives(inputSentence);
console.log(outputSentence); // Output: Green beautiful trees are in the park
```

This function first splits the input sentence into an array of words. It then filters out all the non-alphabetical words and stores them in an array called `adjectives`. Finally, it reverses the order of the adjectives and joins them back into a sentence using a space character. The resulting sentence is returned by the function.

