This error occurs when the user enters an invalid input, such as a non-numeric value, when the program is expecting a numeric input. To handle this exception, you can use a try-catch block to catch the InputMismatchException and prompt the user to enter a valid input. Here's an example of how you can modify your code to handle this exception:
```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        int num1, num2;

        try {
            System.out.print("Enter the first number: ");
            num1 = scanner.nextInt();

            System.out.print("Enter the second number: ");
            num2 = scanner.nextInt();

            // Your code to perform calculations or operations with num1 and num2
        } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter a valid number.");
            scanner.nextLine(); // Consume the remaining input on the line
            main(args); // Recursively call the method to prompt the user for valid input
        }
    }
}
```
In this modified code, we use a try-catch block to handle the InputMismatchException. If the user enters an invalid input, the program will catch the exception and print an error message. It will then consume the remaining input on the line using `scanner.nextLine()` and recursively call the `main` method to prompt the user for valid input. This will continue until the user enters a valid input.

