The Floating-Point Unit (FPU) in the Cortex M4F STM32F407VG MCU is a dedicated hardware component designed for performing floating-point operations. It allows the microcontroller to execute complex mathematical calculations with greater efficiency and accuracy compared to software-based implementations.

How it Works:
The FPU operates by utilizing a set of specialized registers and instructions. When a floating-point operation is executed, the data is loaded into the FPU's registers, and the appropriate instruction is fetched from memory. The FPU then performs the operation using its internal hardware logic, producing the result which is stored back in the register or memory.

How to Use it:
To utilize the FPU in your STM32F407VG project, you need to include the necessary header files and enable the FPU in your code. Here's a simple example of how to use the FPU to perform addition and subtraction:
```c
#include "stm32f4xx_hal.h"

float add_numbers(float num1, float num2) {
    float sum;

    __asm__ volatile (
        "mov r0, %[num1]\n"
        "mov r1, %[num2]\n"
        "add r0, r1\n"
        "mov %[sum], r0\n"
        : [sum] "=r" (sum)
        : [num1] "r" (num1), [num2] "r" (num2)
    );

    return sum;
}

float subtract_numbers(float num1, float num2) {
    float difference;

    __asm__ volatile (
        "mov r0, %[num1]\n"
        "mov r1, %[num2]\n"
        "sub r0, r1\n"
        "mov %[difference], r0\n"
        : [difference] "=r" (difference)
        : [num1] "r" (num1), [num2] "r" (num2)
    );

    return difference;
}

