Here's a JavaScript function that analyzes the given input data and returns the most relevant protocol:

```javascript
function determineProtocol(inputData) {
  const sourceIP = inputData.sourceIP;
  const destinationIP = inputData.destinationIP;
  const port = inputData.port;
  const payload = inputData.payload;

  if (isValidIPv6Address(sourceIP) && isValidIPv6Address(destinationIP)) {
    // Both source and destination IP addresses are in IPv6 format
    if (port === 80) {
      // HTTP protocol is being used for web traffic
      return 'HTTP';
    } else {
      // UDP protocol is being used for other types of traffic
      return 'UDP';
    }
  } else {
    // Either or both source and destination IP addresses are not in IPv6 format
    if (port === 80) {
      // HTTP protocol is being used for web traffic
      return 'HTTP';
    } else {
      // UDP protocol is being used for other types of traffic
      return 'UDP';
    }
  }
}

function isValidIPv6Address(ipAddress) {
  // Regular expression pattern to validate IPv6 address
  const ipv6Regex = /^([0-9A-Fa-f]{1,4}:){7}([0-9A-Fa-f]{1,4})$/;
  return ipv6Regex.test(ipAddress);
}
```

This function first checks if both source and destination IP addresses are in IPv6 format using the `isValidIPv6Address()` function. If they are, it then checks if the port number is 80, which indicates that HTTP protocol is being used for web traffic. If the port number is not 80, it assumes that UDP protocol is being used for other types of traffic. If either or both source and destination IP addresses are not in IPv6 format, it also defaults to returning UDP as the most relevant protocol.

