Here's an example of how you can create a React component using hooks to convert between different temperature units:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const TemperatureConverter = () => {
  const [temperature, setTemperature] = useState(0);
  const [unit, setUnit] = useState('C');

  const handleInputChange = (event) => {
    setTemperature(event.target.value);
  };

  const handleUnitChange = (event) => {
    setUnit(event.target.value);
  };

  const convertToCelsius = () => {
    return (temperature * 9 / 5) + 32;
  };

  const convertToFahrenheit = () => {
    return (temperature - 32) * 5 / 9;
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <input type="number" value={temperature} onChange={handleInputChange} />
      <select value={unit} onChange={handleUnitChange}>
        <option value="C">Celsius</option>
        <option value="F">Fahrenheit</option>
      </select>
      <p>{temperature} {unit === 'C' ? '°C' : '°F'}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setUnit('C')}>Convert to Celsius</button>
      <button onClick={() => setUnit('F')}>Convert to Fahrenheit</button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default TemperatureConverter;
```

This component allows the user to input a temperature value and select a unit (Celsius or Fahrenheit). It then converts the temperature to the other unit and displays the result. The user can also switch between units by clicking on the corresponding button.

To achieve this effect, you'll need to use PHP to calculate the current time and then use CSS to style the sun image accordingly. Here's a sample code:

