To configure the boilerplate files for your Chrome extension to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage, follow these steps:

1. Create a `manifest.json` file in the root directory of your extension with the following content:
```json
{
  "name": "My Extension",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "A simple Chrome extension to inject JavaScript on webpages.",
  "browser_action": {
    "default_icon": "icon.png",
    "default_popup": "popup.html"
  },
  "permissions": ["activeTab", "tabs"]
}
```
2. Create a `popup.html` file in the root directory of your extension with the following content:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <script src="popup.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
  <button id="inject-javascript">Inject JavaScript</button>
</body>
</html>
```
3. Create a `popup.js` file in the root directory of your extension with the following content:
```javascript
document.getElementById("inject-javascript").addEventListener("click", () => {
  chrome.tabs.query({active: true, currentWindow: true}, tabs => {
    chrome.tabs.executeScript(tabs[0].id, {file: "inject.js"});
  });
});
```
4. Create an `inject.js` file in the root directory of your extension with the following content:
```javascript
console.log("Injecting JavaScript...");

// Replace this with your actual JavaScript code to be injected into the webpage
const script = document.createElement("script");
script.src = chrome.runtime.getURL("inject.js");
script.async = true;

document.body.appendChild(script);
```
5. Finally, create an `icon.png` file in the root directory of your extension with a suitable icon for your extension.

Now you can package your extension by creating a folder with the same name as your extension and placing all the files inside it. Then, go to chrome://extensions/ and enable "Developer mode" (top right corner).

