To prevent the planets from being too close together, you can add a simple check for distance between each planet before adding it to the list. If the distance is less than a certain threshold, you can generate a new planet until one with sufficient distance is found. Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

Make sure to pass the desired minimum distance as an argument when calling this function. For example, if you want to ensure a minimum distance of 5 units between planets, you would call the function like this:

```javascript
setPlanets(generateRandomPlanets(5, 5));
```

Here's a simple React utility that removes widows by adjusting the font size. This utility assumes that you have a container element with a fixed width and a dynamic content element inside it.

```javascript
import React, { useRef, useState } from 'react';

const WidowRemover = ({ containerRef, contentRef }) => {
  const [fontSize, setFontSize] = useState(16);

  const containerRefCurrent = useRef(null);
  const contentRefCurrent = useRef(null);

  const handleResize = () => {
    if (containerRefCurrent.current && contentRefCurrent.current) {
      const containerWidth = containerRefCurrent.current.offsetWidth;
      const contentWidth = contentRefCurrent.current.offsetWidth;

      const newFontSize = Math.max(16, containerWidth / contentWidth);

      if (newFontSize !== fontSize) {
        setFontSize(newFontSize);
      }
    }
  };

  return (
    <div ref={containerRef}>
      <div ref={contentRef} style={{ fontSize }}>
        {/* Your dynamic content goes here */}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
};

export default WidowRemover;
```

To use this utility, simply wrap your dynamic content element in the `WidowRemover` component and pass the container and content refs:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import WidowRemover from './WidowRemover';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <div>
      <WidowRemover containerRef={useRef()} contentRef={useRef()} />
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;
```

This will automatically adjust the font size of the dynamic content element to prevent widows. Note that this is a basic implementation and may not work perfectly for all cases. You can customize the logic and styles as needed.

To update your application without replacing the existing EXE, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a new folder on the target machine to store the updated files.
2. Extract the contents of the ZIP file into the new folder.
3. Update the configuration settings (e.g., app.config, web.config) to point to the new location of the updated files.
4. Restart the application to apply the changes.

Here's a more detailed explanation:

1. Create a new folder on the target machine to store the updated files:

Choose a suitable location on the target machine to store the updated files. For example, you can create a new folder in the `C:\Program Files` directory or any other location where you want to store the updates.

```csharp
string updateFolder = Path.Combine(Environment.GetFolderPath(Environment.SpecialFolder.ProgramFiles), "YourApplicationName", "Updates");
if (!Directory.Exists(updateFolder))
{
    Directory.CreateDirectory(updateFolder);
}
```

2. Extract the contents of the ZIP file into the new folder:

Use a library like `System.IO.Compression` to extract the contents of the ZIP file into the new folder.

```csharp
using (ZipArchive zipArchive = ZipFile.OpenRead("path/to/your/zip/file.zip"))
{
    foreach (ZipArchiveEntry entry in zipArchive.Entries)
    {
        string destinationPath = Path.Combine(updateFolder, entry.Name);
        entry.ExtractToFile(destinationPath);
    }
}
```

3. Update the configuration settings (e.g., app.config, web.config) to point to the new location of the updated files:

Update the configuration settings to point to the new location of the updated files. For example, if you have an `app.config` file, you can update the `<application>` tag to point to the new location of the updated files.

```xml
<application>
  <startup>
    <executable>path\to\your\existing\exe</executable>
    <workingDirectory>path\to\your\updated

