Here's a PowerShell script that checks for ACLs referring to SIDs from the SIDHistory of users or groups in the domain:

```powershell
# Define the root folder to start checking
$rootFolder = "C:\YourRootFolder"

# Function to check if an ACL entry refers to a SID in the SIDHistory of a user or group
function CheckACLEntries($folderPath) {
    $acl = Get-Acl -Path $folderPath
    foreach ($access in $acl.Access) {
        if ($access.IdentityReference -match '^S-(?<sid>\w+)$') {
            $sid = $matches['sid']
            $userOrGroup = Get-ADUser -Filter {Sid -eq $sid} -Properties SIDHistory
            if ($userOrGroup) {
                if ($userOrGroup.SIDHistory -contains $sid) {
                    Write-Host "Found ACL entry referring to SID in SIDHistory: $folderPath\-$access.IdentityReference"
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

# Recursively check folders for ACL entries referring to SIDs in the SIDHistory of users or groups
function CheckFolders($folderPath) {
    $subfolders = Get-ChildItem -Path $folderPath -Directory
    foreach ($subfolder in $subfolders) {
        CheckFolders($subfolder.FullName)
    }
    CheckACLEntries($folderPath)
}

# Start checking from the root folder
CheckFolders($rootFolder)
```

Replace `C:\YourRootFolder` with the root folder you want to start checking from. This script will recursively check all subfolders and their ACL entries for references to SIDs in the SIDHistory of users or groups in the domain.
